Subject: [PATCH 2/2] lwcc: fix a self-assignment that loses skipped tokens

The compiler flags it as noise:

  lwcc/preproc.c:1462: explicitly assigning value of variable of type
                       'struct token *' to itself [-Wself-assign]

but it is a real bug, not a redundant line. The loop is collecting the
whitespace tokens it skips while looking for an opening paren, pushing each
onto a list:

      t -> next = t2;
      t2 = t2;          <- never advances the head

`t2 = t;` is the other half of the push. As written the head stays NULL
forever, so the cleanup below -- `while (t2) { ... preproc_unget_token(pp,
t2); ... }` -- never runs, and the skipped tokens are neither ungot nor freed
when the macro turns out not to be function-like.

Scope, stated plainly: this is in **lwcc**, lwtools' C preprocessor, not in
**lwasm**, which is the tool this repo actually builds and uses. Nothing here
exercises the affected path, so the fix is correct by inspection -- it is the
standard push-onto-list idiom, and the cleanup loop below walks the list the
same way -- but it is not verified by running lwcc. It cannot be worse than
the code it replaces, which provably does nothing.

Found building on macOS 2026-08-02.

--- a/lwcc/preproc.c
+++ b/lwcc/preproc.c
@@ -1459,7 +1459,7 @@
 		if (t -> ttype != TOK_WSPACE && t -> ttype != TOK_EOL)
 			break;
 		t -> next = t2;
-		t2 = t2;
+		t2 = t;
 	}
 	if (t -> ttype != TOK_OPAREN)
 	{
